$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>A sale with a long history</h2>
<p>The borrower had taken consortium loans in 1991 secured by the Fernhill resort at Ooty. Recovery proceedings began in 2000; a SARFAESI demand notice followed in 2007 and symbolic possession in 2009, punctuated by tribunal restraint orders. An auction notice issued in March 2010 with a reserve price of Rs. 20 crores; weeks later the Debts Recovery Appellate Tribunal restrained further SARFAESI steps. After the High Court lifted the restraint in September 2011, bids were opened within days, a bidder declared successful — and the sale certificate issued to a partnership firm formed on the very day of the bid-opening. The borrower settled the dues months later, and the lender cancelled the certificate and refunded the consideration, setting up two decades of litigation over whether the auction ever validly happened.</p>

<h2>Five fatal defects</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Bid received under restraint.</strong> A bid was taken while the DRAT&rsquo;s order prohibiting further SARFAESI proceedings subsisted.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Truncated redemption window.</strong> Excluding the thirteen months of restraint, the mandatory thirty-day period under Rule 9(1) had not run its course when the sale was concluded — cutting short the borrower&rsquo;s right of redemption protected by Section 13(8).</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>No notice to the borrower.</strong> After a seventeen-month hiatus, the bids were opened without notice to the borrower.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Certificate to a non-bidder.</strong> The successful bid was an individual&rsquo;s; the certificate went to a newly-formed partnership. Neither the Rules nor the auction terms permitted the substitution.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Missing auction records.</strong> The original records of the auction were never produced despite directions — no proof of the inter-se bidding the auction terms required, nor of written terms for any private-treaty route under Rule 8(8).</div>
</div>

<h2>The principle: mandatory means mandatory</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>The purchaser&rsquo;s case</strong><br>The auction was confirmed, consideration paid and a certificate issued; procedural lapses, if any, were curable irregularities that cannot unsettle a concluded sale years later.</div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>The Court&rsquo;s answer</strong><br>SARFAESI confers the extraordinary power to sell without judicial intervention; its procedural safeguards apply with particular rigour and are substantive protections. A sale vitiated by material irregularity acquires no sanctity from confirmation — sanctity is the reward of legality.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court quashed the judgment under appeal, allowed the borrower&rsquo;s appeals and dismissed the purchaser&rsquo;s, declaring the auction void for breach of mandatory procedure. The contempt petitions were closed, the auction itself being illegal.</p>

<h2>What lenders, borrowers and bidders should take away</h2>
<p>For <strong>secured creditors</strong>, the decision is a compliance checklist: respect every restraint order, recompute timelines after any stay, give fresh notice when a stalled process resumes, document the bidding, and issue the certificate to the bidder alone. For <strong>borrowers</strong>, it confirms that the redemption right under Section 13(8) and the Rule 8–9 safeguards are enforceable substance — and that even a confirmed sale can be undone where the breaches are material. For <strong>auction purchasers</strong>, it is a caution that finality follows only a lawful process; due diligence on the sale procedure itself, not just the title, protects the investment.</p>
<div class="note"><p>Challenges to SARFAESI measures ordinarily go to the Debts Recovery Tribunal under Section 17, not to writ courts — a point Delhi and other High Courts enforce regularly. The present case reached the Supreme Court through that statutory ladder, decades after the sale; the length of the journey is itself an argument for getting the procedure right the first time.</p></div>
